1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,841
This is AgroFlow, by MafiHub.

2
00:00:02,841 --> 00:00:08,396
It helps farmers make better irrigation
decisions from field data,

3
00:00:08,396 --> 00:00:10,500
weather and crop science.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:15,106
A farmer must decide when to irrigate, how
much, for how long,

5
00:00:15,106 --> 00:00:17,378
and whether the weather changes that.

6
00:00:17,378 --> 00:00:21,000
Poor signal, scarce water and uneven
fields make it harder.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,656
AgroFlow combines sensor readings, the
forecast, the crop,

8
00:00:25,656 --> 00:00:30,443
the soil and the irrigation history, using
the FAO-56 crop-water method.

9
00:00:30,443 --> 00:00:32,105
Same inputs, same answer:

10
00:00:32,105 --> 00:00:33,900
it's a calculation, not AI.

11
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,268
Each irrigation zone has a field kit.
Inside the enclosure,

12
00:00:38,268 --> 00:00:42,865
an ESP32 controller reads a YL-69
resistive Soil Moisture Sensor and a DHT11

13
00:00:42,865 --> 00:00:44,800
temperature and humidity sensor.

14
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,417
A relay switches the pump.

15
00:00:47,417 --> 00:00:52,868
The kit reports every thirty seconds,
stops any single run after five minutes,

16
00:00:52,868 --> 00:00:57,900
and if its connection drops, it waters dry
soil in short, rested bursts.

17
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:04,529
Readings travel over secure MQTT to the
AgroFlow API and into a Supabase Postgres

18
00:01:04,529 --> 00:01:05,729
database.

19
00:01:05,729 --> 00:01:08,104
Open-Meteo provides the forecast.

20
00:01:08,104 --> 00:01:12,493
Farmers use the mobile app, administrators
use a web console,

21
00:01:12,493 --> 00:01:14,350
and alerts go out on Telegram.

22
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:21,100
On the farmer's phone, Home gives one
clear action for each irrigation zone.

23
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:23,100
For the tomato field:

24
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:25,237
water about seventy minutes this morning,

25
00:01:25,237 --> 00:01:29,406
roughly seventeen hundred and fifty
litres, or thirty-five millimetres of
water.

26
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:31,300
Confidence is high:

27
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,213
sensor, setup and weather agree.

28
00:01:33,213 --> 00:01:35,664
The spinach beds can wait until Saturday.

29
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,375
AgroFlow estimates how much water the
weather is drawing,

30
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:42,815
five point eight millimetres today.

31
00:01:42,815 --> 00:01:46,302
It adjusts that for the crop and its
growth stage,

32
00:01:46,302 --> 00:01:49,300
so these tomatoes use about six point
four.

33
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:54,451
Soil and root depth set when the crop
starts to struggle.

34
00:01:54,451 --> 00:02:00,100
A daily tally of rain, irrigation and use
puts this field past that point.

35
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:04,379
Each recommendation has a 'Why am I seeing
this?' link:

36
00:02:04,379 --> 00:02:08,300
the inputs, their sources, the rule, and
what would change it.

37
00:02:09,100 --> 00:02:11,660
Here, thirty-five millimetres have been
used,

38
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:14,618
past the thirty-one and a half millimetre
threshold,

39
00:02:14,618 --> 00:02:17,917
and two millimetres of rain won't cover
the day's use. So:

40
00:02:17,917 --> 00:02:18,850
water today.

41
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,963
AgroFlow recommends. The farmer decides.

42
00:02:21,963 --> 00:02:25,550
Here, the farmer applies fifteen hundred
litres instead,

43
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:27,600
and records it with 'I watered'.

44
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,829
AgroFlow counts what actually happened:

45
00:02:30,829 --> 00:02:32,386
no watering needed today.

46
00:02:32,386 --> 00:02:35,500
A soil check by hand can correct the
estimate too.

47
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:40,442
Where a kit controls the pump, a run can
start from the app.

48
00:02:40,442 --> 00:02:42,305
Ninety minutes is rejected:

49
00:02:42,305 --> 00:02:44,100
the limit is one to sixty.

50
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:50,015
Five minutes is confirmed and sent, via
the API and MQTT, to the ESP32,

51
00:02:50,015 --> 00:02:51,600
which starts the pump.

52
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:56,000
The kit reports the run, and it appears in
the history.

53
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:58,191
AgroFlow also looks ahead:

54
00:02:58,191 --> 00:03:01,400
the week-ahead screen says what each day
means for watering.

55
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:05,253
Underneath, the water balance carries on
day by day:

56
00:03:05,253 --> 00:03:08,600
each day's ending state is the next day's
starting point.

57
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,123
So the tomatoes next need water on
Tuesday.

58
00:03:12,123 --> 00:03:13,707
The window rolls forward;

59
00:03:13,707 --> 00:03:15,100
nothing resets weekly.

60
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:17,243
Then the forecast changes:

61
00:03:17,243 --> 00:03:20,289
twenty-two millimetres of rain is now
expected on Saturday,

62
00:03:20,289 --> 00:03:22,200
so AgroFlow recalculates the balance.

63
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,841
The rain refills the root zone:

64
00:03:24,841 --> 00:03:26,800
no irrigation expected this week.

65
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,337
It isn't 'rain means skip'. Too little
rain: still water.

66
00:03:31,337 --> 00:03:34,681
Enough rain: it waits. Some rain: a
smaller amount.

67
00:03:34,681 --> 00:03:37,500
And if the rain fades, watering moves
back.

68
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:42,300
Heavy-rain warnings arrive on Telegram,
with the same 'Why' button.

69
00:03:43,100 --> 00:03:46,500
The soil sensor gives a local signal from
one spot,

70
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:50,100
so AgroFlow treats it as a check, not the
whole truth.

71
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:52,456
Faulty sensors raise warnings;

72
00:03:52,456 --> 00:03:55,100
a farmer's own soil check can correct the
estimate.

73
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:59,181
When setup is incomplete, AgroFlow still
advises.

74
00:03:59,181 --> 00:04:03,600
The maize block has no flow rate, so its
advice is in litres only.

75
00:04:04,400 --> 00:04:07,752
It's marked medium confidence, with one
next step:

76
00:04:07,752 --> 00:04:10,500
time the tap filling a half-litre bottle.

77
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:15,200
Administrators start from the public
website and sign in to the console.

78
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:17,959
Home lists what needs attention.

79
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:23,100
Farmers' requests come in here, and each
zone shows the same advice the farmer
sees.

80
00:04:23,900 --> 00:04:28,751
They can run the engine on demand, monitor
kits, follow up alerts and maintenance,

81
00:04:28,751 --> 00:04:32,300
produce reports, and every administrator
action is recorded.

82
00:04:33,100 --> 00:04:38,114
Field sensing, weather, crop science,
farmer decisions and irrigation history,

83
00:04:38,114 --> 00:04:41,393
combined into practical irrigation
guidance. Sense.

84
00:04:41,393 --> 00:04:42,999
Understand. Act. Monitor.

85
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:46,287
AgroFlow, by MafiHub.

86
00:04:46,287 --> 00:04:52,800
Practical irrigation decisions, grounded
in field data.
